 "document": {
  "id": "D05",
  "path": "conversations/conv_pines019_020_missing_intransit.eml",
  "text": "From: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Missing and damaged items - we need a report\nDate: Thu, 16 Apr 2009 09:00:00 -0500\n\nEvery month I do a shelf reading and mark items as missing or damaged.\nRight now I have no easy way to generate a report of all these items per\nbranch. I need this for my inventory audit and for collection management.\n\nCan we add this?\n\nMaria\n===\nFrom: george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Missing and damaged items - we need a report\nDate: Thu, 16 Apr 2009 09:45:12 -0500\n\nSecond this. We use the data for two purposes:\n1. Auditing - showing how many items went missing/were damaged\n2. Collection decisions - if we keep losing the same titles, maybe we need\n   different copies or to move them to a different location\n\nDo you also need the in-transit items report? I think Maria mentioned\nsomething about delivery issues in her interview notes.\n\nBTW, has anyone seen \"The West Wing\" reruns on Netflix? I'm binge-watching\nit again. Such a great show.\n===\nFrom: dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Missing and damaged items - we need a report\nDate: Thu, 16 Apr 2009 10:20:33 -0500\n\nOh, we definitely need the in-transit report too. We've been losing items\nin the delivery trucks. I need to see what's been in-transit for more than\nX days (X should be configurable, probably 7 days default).\n\nAnd the report should merge transit-to and transit-from so front-line staff\ndon't have to run two reports.\n\nMy neighbor's dog barks at everything. Including my mailbox. The dog has\nissues.\n\n-Dana\n===\nFrom: latoya.davidson@twinlakeslib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Missing and damaged items - we need a report\nDate: Thu, 16 Apr 2009 11:15:00 -0500\n\nI want the missing/damaged report to be per branch. That's crucial for\naccountability. If we see patterns (certain branches losing more items),\nwe can investigate processes.\n\nAlso, can the report include item condition notes? Like \"water damage\" or\n\"vandalism\"? That helps us understand root causes.\n\nTaking my mom to lunch today. She wants to try that new Italian place\ndowntown. I hope it's not too noisy.\n===\nFrom: robert.chen@gapubliclib.org\nTo: latoya.davidson@twinlakeslib.org, dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Missing and damaged items - we need a report\nDate: Thu, 16 Apr 2009 13:42:55 -0500\n\nTechnical note: The system currently tracks item status as \"missing\" or\n\"long overdue\" or various other statuses. We can query for items with\nstatus \"missing\" or \"lost\" and group by branch.\n\nFor in-transit items, we need to check the transit table for items where\nthe arrival date is null and the departure date is more than X days ago.\n\nBoth reports are technically feasible. The missing/damaged report can\ninclude date ranges (e.g., last 30 days, last quarter, etc.) which would\nbe useful for trending.\n\nMy garden tomatoes are finally ripening. The squirrels keep stealing them\nthough. I've named one \"Bandit.\"\n===\nFrom: susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org, latoya.davidson@twinlakeslib.org, robert.chen@gapubliclib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Missing and damaged items + in-transit\nDate: Thu, 16 Apr 2009 14:50:00 -0500\n\nLet me clarify the requirements:\n\n1. MISSING AND DAMAGED ITEMS REPORT:\n   System provides reports of missing and damaged items per branch.\n\n2. IN-TRANSIT ITEMS REPORT:\n   System generates a list of items that have been in-transit for more than\n   X days (X configurable), per branch and per system. The report merges\n   transit-to and transit-from into one report for ease of front-line staff.\n\nThese are two separate but related requirements. Let's track them separately\nin our documentation.\n\nAlso, for the in-transit report - can we sort by due date? Front-line staff\nneed to chase the oldest items first.\n\nWatching \"The Great British Baking Show\" while I work. It's the most\ncomforting show ever created.\n===\nFrom: katherine.gregory@gapubliclib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: SUMMARY: Missing/damaged and in-transit reports\nDate: Fri, 17 Apr 2009 09:15:00 -0500\n\nTwo requirements consolidated:\n\n1. Missing and Damaged Items Report:\n   \"System provides reports of missing and damaged items per branch.\"\n\n2. In-transit Items Report:\n   \"System generates a list of items in-transit for more than X days (X\n   configurable), per branch and per system. Report merges transit-to and\n   transit-from into one report.\"\n\nBoth will be added to the requirements list.\n\nRobert - please confirm technical feasibility by next week.\n\nCoffee. I need coffee.\n\n-Katherine"
 },
 "requirements": [
  {
   "index": 1,
   "name": "Material Volume Reporting",
   "description": "The system must report material volume for a specified library system or branch at the current or a historical date, including total items, new acquisitions, checked-in items, checked-out items, holds, adult fiction, and board books; checked-out items must count toward the total, and category groupings must be configurable by library system, such as fiction/non-fiction or format."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "name": "Acquisition Volume Trends",
   "description": "The system must use item acquisition dates to report material added during selectable periods, including the preceding 30, 60, or 90 days."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "name": "Branch Capacity Overview",
   "description": "The system must provide authorized branch managers with an all-branch capacity view, allow drilldown to an individual branch, show capacity trends such as growth or shrinkage, and support comparison for up to 286 branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 4,
   "name": "Branch Capacity Navigation",
   "description": "The capacity view must allow users to filter and sort branches by region, library type, and current load."
  },
  {
   "index": 5,
   "name": "Capacity Data Export",
   "description": "The system must allow users to export capacity-view data in CSV format."
  },
  {
   "index": 6,
   "name": "Shelf Space Analysis",
   "description": "The system must compare, for each material genre and format, the percentage of total circulations, the percentage of the collection, and the percentage of total shelf space, with views by branch and for the library system, selectable date ranges such as Q1 or Q2, collection percentages based on item counts, circulation percentages based on checkouts, and shelf-space estimates based on item dimensions or item count when dimensions are unavailable."
  },
  {
   "index": 7,
   "name": "Shelf Space Comparisons",
   "description": "The system must show shelf-space analysis for branches side by side, including genre-and-format percentages for circulations, collection holdings, and shelf space, so users can identify differences and outliers."
  },
  {
   "index": 8,
   "name": "Batch Item Transfers",
   "description": "Authorized staff must be able to transfer selected batches of items between branches and other destinations, including mobile libraries, outreach programs, warehouses, book sales, and event locations, by changing item locations or generating pull lists; the operation must process approximately 9.6 million items without excessive database locking."
  },
  {
   "index": 9,
   "name": "Transfer Candidate Queries",
   "description": "Staff must be able to query for candidate items for transfer using criteria such as genre, collection area, and circulation inactivity, including items that have not circulated for six months."
  },
  {
