 "document": {
  "id": "D11",
  "path": "email_financial_reports.eml",
  "text": "From: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 09:15:00 -0500\n\nHi everyone,\n\nI've been working with our finance department at Twin Lakes to understand their\nreporting needs. They've identified several requirements that we should\nincorporate into our specifications. I'm sharing them here for discussion.\n\nKey needs:\n1. Value of items report - staff should be able to get the value of items\n   based on data in item records. This could be the entire collection, or a\n   portion. For example, they want to be able to get:\n   - Value of all items with status \"longoverdue\"\n   - Value of all DVDs in children's collection\n   - Value of entire collection\n\n2. Financial reports - staff need reports on:\n   - Patron account balances by patron, home library, and system\n   - Fines and charges accrued per time period (last 12 months, YTD, last month)\n   - Fines waived per time period and per branch\n   - Payments made per time period and per payment method (staff desk,\n     self-check, OPAC)\n\n3. Audit trail - system must maintain a ledger of patron payments, including\n   which charges payments are applied to, to facilitate reconciliation.\n\n4. Compliance - all reports and data archiving must comply with standard\n   accounting practice and state, county, and municipal auditing requirements.\n\nThoughts?\n\nLaToya\n===\nFrom: george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org\nTo: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 10:30:22 -0500\n\nLaToya,\n\nThese are spot on. I want to add a few things from our perspective:\n\n- The financial audit trail is CRITICAL for our finance department. They need\n  to be able to trace every payment back to the specific charge it was applied\n  to. Without this, we can't reconcile our accounts properly.\n\n- Regarding the \"value of items report\" - the data is already in the item\n  records (purchase price field). We just need the system to be able to query\n  and aggregate this. This should be straightforward.\n\n- On compliance - we need to ensure that data archiving follows state and local\n  requirements. Some data needs to be retained for 7 years for audit purposes.\n  The system needs to enforce retention policies.\n\nGeorge\n===\nFrom: susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org\nTo: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 11:45:01 -0500\n\nI agree with everything. A few additional points:\n\n- The financial data is attached to patron records AND item records. So when\n  we query for financial reports, we need to be able to join these data sources.\n\n- For fines and charges, the staff should be able to query patron accounts\n  with balances greater than X dollars. This is useful for identifying accounts\n  that need attention.\n\n- The financial reports need to show payments by method - cash, credit card,\n  check, etc. This helps with daily reconciliation.\n\n- Fines waived should be tracked per library because each library has its own\n  budget for waived fines.\n\nSusan\n===\nFrom: dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org\nTo: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 14:20:33 -0500\n\nAdding from the staff perspective:\n\n- We need to easily query patron accounts with balances greater than X dollars.\n  Right now it takes forever to find these accounts manually.\n\n- Financial information needs to be easily updatable. When a patron makes a\n  payment at the front desk, we need the system to reflect that immediately.\n\n- Payments tracking by library is important for our end-of-month reports. Each\n  branch needs to be able to see what payments were collected at their location.\n\nDana\n===\nFrom: katherine.gregory@gapubliclib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: SUMMARY: Financial reporting requirements - consolidated list\nDate: Tue, 14 Jul 2009 08:45:00 -0500\n\nThanks everyone for the detailed discussion. Here's my consolidated summary of\nfinancial reporting requirements:\n\n1. VALUE OF ITEMS REPORT:\n   System can generate a report of the value of items (based on data in item\n   record) in the entire collection or a portion. Examples: value of all items\n   with status of \"longoverdue\", value of all DVDs in children's collection,\n   value of entire collection.\n\n2. COMPLIANCE:\n   All reports and data archiving must comply with standard accounting practice\n   and state, county, and municipal auditing requirements.\n\n3. FINANCIAL DATA (PATRONS):\n   Fines, charges, waivers, and ecommerce transactions are attached to patron\n   and item records. System tracks fines waived and payments made per library.\n   Financial information can be updated easily. A staff user can easily query\n   patron accounts with balances greater than X dollars.\n\n4. FINANCIAL REPORTS:\n   The system provides financial reports including:\n   - Patron account balances by patron, home library, and system\n   - Fines and charges accrued per time period (last 12 months, YTD, last month)\n     and per type of charge (overdue fines, damaged item charges, lost item\n     charges, etc.)\n   - Fines waived per time period and per branch\n   - Payments made per time period and per payment method (staff desk,\n     self-check station, OPAC)\n\n5. AUDIT TRAIL:\n   The system maintains a ledger of patron payments, including which charges\n   payments are applied to, to facilitate reconciliation.\n\nI'll add these to the master requirements list.\n\n- Katherine"
 },
 "requirements": [
  {
   "index": 1,
   "name": "Material Volume Reporting",
   "description": "The system must report material volume for a specified library system or branch at the current or a historical date, including total items, new acquisitions, checked-in items, checked-out items, holds, adult fiction, and board books; checked-out items must count toward the total, and category groupings must be configurable by library system, such as fiction/non-fiction or format."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "name": "Acquisition Volume Trends",
   "description": "The system must use item acquisition dates to report material added during selectable periods, including the preceding 30, 60, or 90 days."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "name": "Branch Capacity Overview",
   "description": "The system must provide authorized branch managers with an all-branch capacity view, allow drilldown to an individual branch, show capacity trends such as growth or shrinkage, and support comparison for up to 286 branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 4,
   "name": "Branch Capacity Navigation",
   "description": "The capacity view must allow users to filter and sort branches by region, library type, and current load."
  },
  {
   "index": 5,
   "name": "Capacity Data Export",
   "description": "The system must allow users to export capacity-view data in CSV format."
  },
  {
   "index": 6,
   "name": "Shelf Space Analysis",
   "description": "The system must compare, for each material genre and format, the percentage of total circulations, the percentage of the collection, and the percentage of total shelf space, with views by branch and for the library system, selectable date ranges such as Q1 or Q2, collection percentages based on item counts, circulation percentages based on checkouts, and shelf-space estimates based on item dimensions or item count when dimensions are unavailable."
  },
  {
   "index": 7,
   "name": "Shelf Space Comparisons",
   "description": "The system must show shelf-space analysis for branches side by side, including genre-and-format percentages for circulations, collection holdings, and shelf space, so users can identify differences and outliers."
  },
  {
   "index": 8,
   "name": "Batch Item Transfers",
   "description": "Authorized staff must be able to transfer selected batches of items between branches and other destinations, including mobile libraries, outreach programs, warehouses, book sales, and event locations, by changing item locations or generating pull lists; the operation must process approximately 9.6 million items without excessive database locking."
  },
  {
   "index": 9,
   "name": "Transfer Candidate Queries",
   "description": "Staff must be able to query for candidate items for transfer using criteria such as genre, collection area, and circulation inactivity, including items that have not circulated for six months."
  },
  {
   "index": 10,
   "name": "Saved Transfer Queries",
   "description": "Staff must be able to save transfer-candidate queries for repeated use, including monthly reuse, and make saved queries available for reuse or sharing."
  },
  {
   "index": 11,
   "name": "Transfer Item Selection",
   "description": "Staff must be able to manually select individual titles or items from transfer-query results before executing a transfer, such as selecting 25 items from 100 candidates."
  },
  {
   "index": 12,
   "name": "Transfer Location Updates",
   "description": "The system must update the location field and associated item records for selected items moved to a new location."
  },
  {
   "index": 13,
   "name": "Transfer Pull Lists",
   "description": "The system must generate printable pull lists identifying items selected for transfer."
  },
  {
   "index": 14,
   "name": "Transfer Reversion",
   "description": "The system must return transferred items to their original locations after a configured period or configured event, including a status change to sold or returned or checkout at the destination."
  },
  {
   "index": 15,
   "name": "Transfer Audit History",
   "description": "The system must record transfer history for each item, including enough history to determine patterns such as five transfers within one year."
  },
  {
   "index": 16,
   "name": "Transfer Confirmation Preview",
   "description": "Before execution, the system must show a transfer preview containing the item count and source and destination locations and require staff confirmation."
  },
  {
   "index": 17,
   "name": "Transfer Undo",
   "description": "Staff must be able to undo an erroneous transfer after it has been initiated or completed."
  },
  {
   "index": 18,
   "name": "Transfer Role Permissions",
   "description": "The system must restrict transfers to authorized roles, including managers, administrators, and transfer coordinators."
  },
  {
   "index": 19,
   "name": "Transit Label Generation",
   "description": "The system must generate printable barcode transit labels for transferred items."
  },
  {
   "index": 20,
   "name": "Transfer Delay Alerts",
   "description": "The system must notify designated staff when a transfer is delayed or encounters an issue."
  },
  {
   "index": 21,
   "name": "Missing Damaged Reports",
   "description": "The system must report missing and damaged items by branch, including condition notes such as water damage or vandalism and selectable periods such as the preceding 30 days or last quarter."
  },
  {
   "index": 22,
   "name": "Overdue Transit Report",
   "description": "The system must provide one report combining transit-to and transit-from items that have remained in transit longer than a configurable threshold, defaulting to seven days where specified, using a null arrival date and an overdue departure date; results must be available by branch and library system and sortable by due date."
  },
  {
   "index": 23,
   "name": "Purge Candidate Identification",
   "description": "The system must identify item records eligible for purging using customizable status, duration, and circulation criteria, including missing, discard/weed, or damaged status, time in status, and no circulation for a specified period; criteria must support Boolean combinations such as missing for six months and no circulation for two years."
  },
  {
   "index": 24,
   "name": "Batch Status Conversion",
   "description": "The system must provide a report or workflow for converting many selected items from one status to another in one batch, such as changing 100 missing items to discard/weed."
  },
  {
   "index": 25,
   "name": "Last Copy Reporting",
   "description": "The system must report items that are the only copy within a selected branch, library system, consortium, bibliographic work, or collection grouping, including deleted last copies where applicable, so catalogers can update WorldCat and avoid inappropriate deletion."
  },
  {
   "index": 26,
   "name": "Orphan Record Deletion",
   "description": "The system must provide a quick method to delete volume entries with their associated copy entries and records with no attached items."
  },
  {
   "index": 27,
   "name": "Protected Purge Reports",
   "description": "Reports identifying purge candidates must be read-only and must not delete or modify candidate records."
  },
  {
   "index": 28,
   "name": "Confirmed Purge Deletion",
   "description": "The system must require a separate confirmation before executing purge deletions."
  },
  {
   "index": 29,
   "name": "Purge Audit Trail",
   "description": "The system must record who purged each record and when."
  },
  {
   "index": 30,
   "name": "Deletion Audit Logging",
   "description": "The system must record completed and denied deletion attempts, including record type, record ID, timestamp, user, user branch, library system, and, where required, deletion reason; deletion data must support counts and queries by branch, system, date range, user, record type, and highest-volume users."
  },
  {
   "index": 31,
   "name": "Patron Report Filtering",
   "description": "The system must filter and group patron queries and reports by age range derived from birth date, ZIP code, county, home branch, patron type, and preferred language, and must combine filters with AND and OR logic and applicable circulation conditions."
  },
  {
   "index": 32,
   "name": "Configurable Age Ranges",
   "description": "Users must be able to configure age ranges separately for each patron report, including ranges such as 0–5, 6–10, 11–15 or 0–6, 7–12, 13–17, 18–24, and 25–34."
  },
  {
   "index": 33,
   "name": "Null Demographic Inclusion",
   "description": "Patron queries and reports must include patrons whose optional demographic fields are null or blank rather than silently excluding them."
  },
  {
   "index": 34,
   "name": "Inactive Patron Reporting",
   "description": "The system must report patrons with no circulation and no electronic activity during a configurable preceding period, including OPAC or patron-account logins, online holds, online renewals, and integrated digital-resource access; the threshold must support any number of days, including 30, 90, 180, and 365."
  },
  {
   "index": 35,
   "name": "Inactive Report Scope",
   "description": "The inactive patron report must list or group results by configurable organizational scopes, including library system, home branch, and county, so users can see system totals and assign work to branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 36,
   "name": "Inactive Patron Outreach",
   "description": "The inactive patron report must include each patron's current mailing address and support CSV export for upload to an outreach system."
  },
  {
   "index": 37,
   "name": "Inactive Patron Exceptions",
   "description": "The inactive patron report must separate patrons with outstanding fines and allow exclusion of do-not-disturb patrons, VIP patrons, staff members, and patrons who opted out of communications."
  },
  {
   "index": 38,
   "name": "Scalable Inactive Reporting",
   "description": "For environments with approximately 3.5 million patrons, inactive patron reports must run as background operations and cache slow activity-query results for reuse."
  },
  {
   "index": 39,
   "name": "Consortium Scale",
   "description": "The system must support a library consortium with 286 branches, 17 million annual circulations, and purchasing and processing exceeding 900,000 items per year."
  },
  {
   "index": 40,
   "name": "Open-Hours Performance",
   "description": "Searches and reports must run during open or peak hours without disrupting circulation, other system functions, or the OPAC; background queues, read-only replicas, or equivalent isolation must avoid excessive database contention."
  },
  {
   "index": 41,
   "name": "Server Platform Support",
   "description": "The system must operate on Linux and Solaris servers, including Red Hat Enterprise Linux 5 and Solaris 10 on SPARC hardware where specified."
  },
  {
   "index": 42,
   "name": "Client And Browser Access",
   "description": "The system must be accessible through a web browser or Windows-compatible client and support Microsoft Internet Explorer 6.0 or later and Mozilla Firefox 2.0 or later."
  },
  {
   "index": 43,
   "name": "Assistive Technology Access",
   "description": "The system must satisfy Section 508 accessibility requirements and support screen readers including JAWS and NVDA, screen magnification including ZoomText, and other assistive technologies; web output must include appropriate alt tags, ARIA labels, semantic structure, and standards-compliant HTML."
  },
  {
   "index": 44,
   "name": "Relational Database Support",
   "description": "The system must use and operate with a fully relational database, support complex joins, and not require a denormalized reporting schema that can cause synchronization problems."
  },
  {
   "index": 45,
   "name": "Standards Compliant HTML",
   "description": "Web interfaces, reports, and online help must produce HTML that validates against applicable web standards."
  },
  {
   "index": 46,
   "name": "Environment Separation",
   "description": "The product must provide separate development and training environments, including a training environment safe for experimentation without affecting production."
  },
  {
   "index": 47,
   "name": "Configuration Migration",
   "description": "The system must export and import report templates, permission settings, saved queries, and other configuration between development, training, and production environments."
  },
  {
   "index": 48,
   "name": "Role Based Access Control",
   "description": "Administrators must control rights through security groups and roles assigned to individuals, workgroups, and arbitrary staff groups; changing a role assignment must change permissions. Supported levels include Global Admin, Local Admin, Library Manager, Staff, and indirect Patron access through the OPAC, with report and transfer permissions appropriate to each role."
  },
  {
   "index": 49,
   "name": "OPAC Preservation",
   "description": "Management and reporting functionality must not constrain, slow, or adversely affect OPAC functionality or patron access."
  },
  {
   "index": 50,
   "name": "Record Data Specifications",
   "description": "The product must provide complete data specifications and dictionaries for authority, bibliographic, order, item, hold/request, and other accessed records, including field names, data types, and relationships."
  },
  {
   "index": 51,
   "name": "Process Documentation",
   "description": "The product must document major processes, including bibliographic import and export, validation against internal and external authority sources, and standard reports."
  },
  {
   "index": 52,
   "name": "Online System Help",
   "description": "The system must provide hierarchical, cross-linked, searchable HTML help that describes and illustrates system functions and is accessible within the system and to screen readers."
  },
  {
   "index": 53,
   "name": "Error Feedback",
   "description": "The system must provide high-level error reporting and low-level query-specific feedback to administrators, including relevant table, field, and error type when available."
  },
  {
   "index": 54,
   "name": "Staff Authentication",
   "description": "The system must support optional employee-card or badge login and individual username-and-password login as a fallback; every login must identify the individual staff member and card login must not bypass individual authentication."
  },
  {
   "index": 55,
   "name": "Report Template Controls",
   "description": "Authorized administrators must create shared report templates that staff can run as-is or modify within controlled limits on filters and display fields, without compromising intended results or exposing deleted items; authorized users must be able to clone templates and edit permitted display options."
  },
  {
   "index": 56,
   "name": "Report Permission Administration",
   "description": "The system must provide fine-grained permissions for creating, cloning, running, and accessing reports, filters, data sets, and shared folders, including administrator-controlled shared folders that allow or prohibit specific report access."
  },
  {
   "index": 57,
   "name": "Multi Record Query Designer",
   "description": "The system must provide a user-friendly query designer across bibliographic, item, patron, transaction, financial, and other record types, with selectable fields, picklists, regular-expression choices, Boolean operators, and administrator control over tables, fields, and tool access."
  },
  {
   "index": 58,
   "name": "Executive Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide predefined reports for library boards and executive users, including Georgia Annual Report and Application for State Aid statistics and traffic and usage cross-tabulations by terminal, branch, system, hour, age range, patron type, and patron location."
  },
  {
   "index": 59,
   "name": "Privacy Preserving Archives",
   "description": "The system must retain transaction data for the legally required period while removing personally identifying patron information, preserving useful demographic statistics, and retaining monthly and annual aggregate information indefinitely where required."
  },
  {
   "index": 60,
   "name": "Periodic Management Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide periodic reports for records with holds, items not checked out for a configurable period, item-level holds, invalid item types, in-transit items with holds, overdue transit, last copies, long call numbers, missing items, bibliographic records without items, and patrons with invalid home branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 61,
   "name": "Query Criteria Display",
   "description": "Report results must display the query selections, report name, and selected date criteria used to produce them."
  },
  {
   "index": 62,
   "name": "Report Queue Monitoring",
   "description": "Users must be able to see report queue contents, position, number of reports ahead, submission time, and status such as waiting, running, completed, or failed."
  },
  {
   "index": 63,
   "name": "Patron Behavior Statistics",
   "description": "The system must produce statistics about patron behavior and material use, including return speed, renewal likelihood, renewal rates, and the percentage of branch checkouts that are renewals."
  },
  {
   "index": 64,
   "name": "Anonymized Demographic Statistics",
