{
 "document": {
  "id": "D09",
  "path": "conversations/conv_pines024_inactive_patrons.eml",
  "text": "From: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Inactive patrons report needed\nDate: Tue, 05 May 2009 08:45:00 -0500\n\nWe need a report that shows patrons with no circulation or electronic\nactivity in the last X days. X should be configurable - we usually use 365\ndays but it would be nice to adjust.\n\nThe list should be configurable to different levels - System, Home Library,\nCounty, etc.\n\nWho else needs this?\n\nMaria\n(Also, anyone have a good eye cream recommendation? I'm starting to see\nfine lines and it's freaking me out.)\n===\nFrom: george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Inactive patrons report needed\nDate: Tue, 05 May 2009 09:20:00 -0500\n\nThis is huge for us. We run this quarterly to clean up our patron database.\nInactive records bloat the database and slow things down.\n\nFor the configurable levels - I'd want System and Home Library. At the\nsystem level, I can see which branches have the most inactive patrons.\nAt the home library level, I can assign cleanup tasks to specific branches.\n\nCan we also include \"electronic activity\"? That means online account\naccess, not just physical checkouts. Some patrons use the OPAC but don't\ncheck out physical items anymore.\n\nMy son just got accepted to Georgia Tech. I'm so proud!\n===\nFrom: dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Inactive patrons report needed\nDate: Tue, 05 May 2009 10:15:00 -0500\n\nWe use this for outreach - trying to re-engage patrons who haven't visited\nin a while. We mail postcards to inactive patrons, so the report needs to\ninclude current mailing addresses.\n\nWhat counts as \"electronic activity\"? Is it just logging into the OPAC?\nOr also accessing digital resources (OverDrive, etc.)?\n\nAlso, should we flag patrons who are inactive but still owe fines? We\nshould probably handle those differently.\n\nMy daughter's first piano recital is this weekend. She's been practicing\nChopin for months. I'm nervous just watching her practice.\n\n-Dana\n===\nFrom: susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org\nTo: maria.rodeguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Inactive patrons report needed (note typo correction)\nDate: Tue, 05 May 2009 10:45:00 -0500\n\nDana raises a good point about what counts as activity. Let me clarify:\n\n\"Electronic activity\" should include:\n1. Logging into OPAC/patron account\n2. Placing holds online\n3. Renewing items online\n4. Accessing digital collections (if integrated)\n\nAnd yes, we should track inactive patrons who still owe fines separately.\nThose accounts need different handling.\n\nFor the configurable X days - what ranges make sense?\n- 30 days (monthly check)\n- 90 days (quarterly check)\n- 180 days (semi-annual)\n- 365 days (annual)\n\nCan we make it fully customizable? Like any number of days?\n\nI just realized I've been pronouncing \"espresso\" wrong my whole life.\nI've been saying \"expresso.\" Mind blown.\n===\nFrom: robert.chen@gapubliclib.org\nTo: susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org, dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Inactive patrons report needed\nDate: Tue, 05 May 2009 11:30:00 -0500\n\nTechnically, this is straightforward. We query the transaction table for\nthe most recent activity per patron, and filter for those whose last\nactivity is more than X days ago.\n\nFor \"electronic activity,\" we track:\n- OPAC login events\n- Online hold placements\n- Online renewals\n- Digital resource access (if logged)\n\nThese are stored in different tables, so we'd need a combined query.\n\nFor the configurable levels - System, Home Library, County - that's just\nGROUP BY clauses. Easy.\n\nOne performance note: for 3.5 million patrons, this query could be slow.\nWe should run it as a background report and cache results.\n\nThe power went out for 2 hours this morning. I had to work on my laptop\non battery. Very slow going.\n===\nFrom: latoya.davidson@twinlakeslib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org, susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org, robert.chen@gapubliclib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Inactive patrons report needed\nDate: Tue, 05 May 2009 12:00:00 -0500\n\nOne more thing - we should be able to EXCLUDE certain patrons from the\ninactive report. For example:\n- Patrons with \"do not disturb\" flags\n- VIP patrons\n- Staff members\n- Patrons who have opted out of communications\n\nAlso, the report should be exportable. We upload it to our outreach system.\n\nMy garden is finally blooming. The peonies are enormous this year.\n===\nFrom: katherine.gregory@gapubliclib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: SUMMARY: Inactive patrons report requirement\nDate: Wed, 06 May 2009 09:15:00 -0500\n\nRequirement consolidated:\n\nThe system should generate a list of patrons with no circulation or\nelectronic activity in the last X days (X configurable). Electronic activity\nincludes OPAC login, online hold placement, online renewals.\n\nThe list should be configurable to:\n- System\n- Home Library\n- County\n- (Other levels as needed)\n\nAdditional considerations:\n- Include current mailing addresses for outreach\n- Track inactive patrons with outstanding fines separately\n- Allow exclusion of certain patron types\n- Exportable format (CSV)\n- Background processing for performance\n\nAction: Robert will optimize the query. Susan and Dana will provide test\nscenarios for outreach.\n\n-Katherine\n(P.S. \"Italian\" is \"ee-tal-EE-an.\" You're welcome, Susan.)"
 },
 "requirements": [
  {
   "index": 1,
   "name": "Material Volume Reporting",
   "description": "The system must report material volume for a specified library system or branch at the current or a historical date, including total items, new acquisitions, checked-in items, checked-out items, holds, adult fiction, and board books; checked-out items must count toward the total, and category groupings must be configurable by library system, such as fiction/non-fiction or format."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "name": "Acquisition Volume Trends",
   "description": "The system must use item acquisition dates to report material added during selectable periods, including the preceding 30, 60, or 90 days."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "name": "Branch Capacity Overview",
   "description": "The system must provide authorized branch managers with an all-branch capacity view, allow drilldown to an individual branch, show capacity trends such as growth or shrinkage, and support comparison for up to 286 branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 4,
   "name": "Branch Capacity Navigation",
   "description": "The capacity view must allow users to filter and sort branches by region, library type, and current load."
  },
  {
   "index": 5,
   "name": "Capacity Data Export",
   "description": "The system must allow users to export capacity-view data in CSV format."
  },
  {
   "index": 6,
   "name": "Shelf Space Analysis",
   "description": "The system must compare, for each material genre and format, the percentage of total circulations, the percentage of the collection, and the percentage of total shelf space, with views by branch and for the library system, selectable date ranges such as Q1 or Q2, collection percentages based on item counts, circulation percentages based on checkouts, and shelf-space estimates based on item dimensions or item count when dimensions are unavailable."
  },
  {
