  "text": "From: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Can we see all branches at once?\nDate: Wed, 08 Apr 2009 09:30:00 -0500\n\nHi team,\n\nAs a branch manager, I need to see the capacity of all branches in our\nsystem, not just my own. When we're deciding where to move items or\nallocate resources, it helps to see the big picture.\n\nIs there a way to see an overview of all branches' capacity?\n\nMaria\n===\nFrom: george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Can we see all branches at once?\nDate: Wed, 08 Apr 2009 10:15:33 -0500\n\nYes! This is so needed. Last month we had to move 200 books from Piedmont\nto three other branches because our HVAC broke. It would have been so much\neasier if I could have seen at a glance which branches had capacity.\n\nI'm also dealing with a mouse problem in the staff area. Anyone have a good\nexterminator recommendation?\n\nGeorge\n===\nFrom: chris.sharp@gapubliclib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Can we see all branches at once?\nDate: Wed, 08 Apr 2009 11:00:12 -0500\n\nThis reminds me of the capacity interface from the King County spec. It\nwas basically a dashboard showing all libraries' capacity at once.\n\nI think we need:\n1. A high-level summary view (all branches, key metrics)\n2. Ability to drill down into individual branches\n3. Maybe color-coding - green for under capacity, yellow for getting full,\n   red for at/over capacity\n\nMy kid's soccer practice got rained out again. Third time this week.\n===\nFrom: robert.chen@gapubliclib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, chris.sharp@gapubliclib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Can we see all branches at once?\nDate: Wed, 08 Apr 2009 14:22:45 -0500\n\nFrom a technical standpoint, this is doable. The data is centralized in\nour database, so we can build queries that aggregate across all branches.\n\nOne consideration: with 286 locations, the overview might get crowded.\nMaybe we should group by system/region? Or allow filtering?\n\nAlso, what specific metrics define \"capacity\"? Is it:\n- Number of items vs. shelf space?\n- Number of physical items vs. a configured limit?\n- Circulation volume vs. processing capacity?\n\nNeed to nail this down before building the interface.\n\nP.S. My cat has started sleeping on my keyboard while I work. Very\ndistracting but also kind of adorable.\n===\nFrom: jennifer.durham@statesboro.lib.org\nTo: maria.rodriguez@dalton-whitman.lib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, chris.sharp@gapubliclib.org, robert.chen@gapubliclib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Can we see all branches at once?\nDate: Wed, 08 Apr 2009 15:40:18 -0500\n\nI'd want to be able to sort and filter. By region, by library type, by\ncurrent load... And export to CSV for the directors who want spreadsheets.\n\nOne more thing - can the capacity interface also show trends? Like, is\nthis branch growing or shrinking? That helps with long-term planning.\n\nMy mom sent me cookies in the mail today. Chocolate chip. She still\nremembers how I like them.\n===\nFrom: katherine.gregory@gapubliclib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: SUMMARY: System capacity interface\nDate: Thu, 09 Apr 2009 09:00:00 -0500\n\nQuick summary on the system capacity interface discussion:\n\nThe system should provide an interface showing the capacity of all branches.\nThis should include:\n- High-level overview of all branches' capacity at once\n- Ability to drill down into individual branches\n- Sorting and filtering capabilities (region, type, load)\n- Trend data (growth/shrink patterns)\n- Export to CSV\n\nRobert will look into technical constraints around displaying 286 locations.\nChris will draft initial mockups.\n\nNow someone please tell me where I can buy a decent cup of coffee around\nhere.\n\n-Katherine"
 },
