 "document": {
  "id": "D11",
  "path": "email_financial_reports.eml",
  "text": "From: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 09:15:00 -0500\n\nHi everyone,\n\nI've been working with our finance department at Twin Lakes to understand their\nreporting needs. They've identified several requirements that we should\nincorporate into our specifications. I'm sharing them here for discussion.\n\nKey needs:\n1. Value of items report - staff should be able to get the value of items\n   based on data in item records. This could be the entire collection, or a\n   portion. For example, they want to be able to get:\n   - Value of all items with status \"longoverdue\"\n   - Value of all DVDs in children's collection\n   - Value of entire collection\n\n2. Financial reports - staff need reports on:\n   - Patron account balances by patron, home library, and system\n   - Fines and charges accrued per time period (last 12 months, YTD, last month)\n   - Fines waived per time period and per branch\n   - Payments made per time period and per payment method (staff desk,\n     self-check, OPAC)\n\n3. Audit trail - system must maintain a ledger of patron payments, including\n   which charges payments are applied to, to facilitate reconciliation.\n\n4. Compliance - all reports and data archiving must comply with standard\n   accounting practice and state, county, and municipal auditing requirements.\n\nThoughts?\n\nLaToya\n===\nFrom: george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org\nTo: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 10:30:22 -0500\n\nLaToya,\n\nThese are spot on. I want to add a few things from our perspective:\n\n- The financial audit trail is CRITICAL for our finance department. They need\n  to be able to trace every payment back to the specific charge it was applied\n  to. Without this, we can't reconcile our accounts properly.\n\n- Regarding the \"value of items report\" - the data is already in the item\n  records (purchase price field). We just need the system to be able to query\n  and aggregate this. This should be straightforward.\n\n- On compliance - we need to ensure that data archiving follows state and local\n  requirements. Some data needs to be retained for 7 years for audit purposes.\n  The system needs to enforce retention policies.\n\nGeorge\n===\nFrom: susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org\nTo: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 11:45:01 -0500\n\nI agree with everything. A few additional points:\n\n- The financial data is attached to patron records AND item records. So when\n  we query for financial reports, we need to be able to join these data sources.\n\n- For fines and charges, the staff should be able to query patron accounts\n  with balances greater than X dollars. This is useful for identifying accounts\n  that need attention.\n\n- The financial reports need to show payments by method - cash, credit card,\n  check, etc. This helps with daily reconciliation.\n\n- Fines waived should be tracked per library because each library has its own\n  budget for waived fines.\n\nSusan\n===\nFrom: dana.peeler@ohoopeelib.org\nTo: la.toyadavidson@twinlakeslib.org, george.tuttle@piedmontlib.org, susansexton-cooley@saraboblib.org, pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nSubject: Re: Financial reporting requirements - discussion thread\nDate: Mon, 13 Jul 2009 14:20:33 -0500\n\nAdding from the staff perspective:\n\n- We need to easily query patron accounts with balances greater than X dollars.\n  Right now it takes forever to find these accounts manually.\n\n- Financial information needs to be easily updatable. When a patron makes a\n  payment at the front desk, we need the system to reflect that immediately.\n\n- Payments tracking by library is important for our end-of-month reports. Each\n  branch needs to be able to see what payments were collected at their location.\n\nDana\n===\nFrom: katherine.gregory@gapubliclib.org\nTo: pines-reports-working-group@gapl.org\nCc: finance.committee@gapl.org\nSubject: SUMMARY: Financial reporting requirements - consolidated list\nDate: Tue, 14 Jul 2009 08:45:00 -0500\n\nThanks everyone for the detailed discussion. Here's my consolidated summary of\nfinancial reporting requirements:\n\n1. VALUE OF ITEMS REPORT:\n   System can generate a report of the value of items (based on data in item\n   record) in the entire collection or a portion. Examples: value of all items\n   with status of \"longoverdue\", value of all DVDs in children's collection,\n   value of entire collection.\n\n2. COMPLIANCE:\n   All reports and data archiving must comply with standard accounting practice\n   and state, county, and municipal auditing requirements.\n\n3. FINANCIAL DATA (PATRONS):\n   Fines, charges, waivers, and ecommerce transactions are attached to patron\n   and item records. System tracks fines waived and payments made per library.\n   Financial information can be updated easily. A staff user can easily query\n   patron accounts with balances greater than X dollars.\n\n4. FINANCIAL REPORTS:\n   The system provides financial reports including:\n   - Patron account balances by patron, home library, and system\n   - Fines and charges accrued per time period (last 12 months, YTD, last month)\n     and per type of charge (overdue fines, damaged item charges, lost item\n     charges, etc.)\n   - Fines waived per time period and per branch\n   - Payments made per time period and per payment method (staff desk,\n     self-check station, OPAC)\n\n5. AUDIT TRAIL:\n   The system maintains a ledger of patron payments, including which charges\n   payments are applied to, to facilitate reconciliation.\n\nI'll add these to the master requirements list.\n\n- Katherine"
 },
 "requirements": [
  {
   "index": 1,
   "name": "Collection And Material Use Reports",
   "description": "The reports interface must enable authorized managers, administrators, and staff to analyze collections and material use for planning, evaluation, and oversight."
  },
  {
   "index": 2,
   "name": "Consortium Scale",
   "description": "The system must support a library consortium with 286 branches, 17 million annual circulations, and purchasing and processing exceeding 900,000 items per year."
  },
  {
   "index": 3,
   "name": "Cross-Branch Performance Reports",
   "description": "The reports interface must enable analysis of performance across branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 4,
   "name": "Role-Based Transfer Access",
   "description": "Administrators must control rights through security groups and roles assigned to individuals, workgroups, and arbitrary staff groups, with changes to role assignments changing permissions. The library automation system must support Global Admin, Local Admin, Library Manager, Staff, and indirect Patron access through the OPAC, with report and transfer permissions appropriate to each role and transfers restricted to authorized roles, including managers, administrators, and transfer coordinators."
  },
  {
   "index": 5,
   "name": "Collection Analysis Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide reports on weeding data, collection age, collection value, and last-copy status. Collection-value reports must use purchase-price or other item-record data and cover the entire collection or selected portions, including status- or collection-based selections such as longoverdue items or children's DVDs. Last-copy reports must identify items that are the only copy within a selected branch, library system, consortium, bibliographic work, or collection grouping, including deleted last copies where applicable, so catalogers can update WorldCat and avoid inappropriate deletion."
  },
  {
   "index": 6,
   "name": "Item Status and Activity Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide reports on items that are missing, overdue, in transit, claimed returned, selected by use count, or associated with a fines-stopped reason."
  },
  {
   "index": 7,
   "name": "Missing Damaged Reports",
   "description": "The system must report missing and damaged items by branch, including condition notes such as water damage or vandalism and selectable periods such as the preceding 30 days or last quarter."
  },
  {
   "index": 8,
   "name": "Transit Report Suite",
   "description": "The system must provide reports for items in transit to a destination branch and items in transit by sending branch, including one report combining transit-to and transit-from items that have remained in transit longer than a configurable threshold, defaulting to seven days where specified, using a null arrival date and an overdue departure date; results must be available by branch and library system and sortable by due date."
  },
  {
   "index": 9,
   "name": "Activity and Traffic Cross-Tabulation",
   "description": "The system must provide activity, traffic, and usage reports showing counts of checkouts, check-ins, holds placed, and holds filled, cross-tabulated by terminal, branch, library system, hour, patron age range, patron type, and patron location, and must classify hold-request counts by placement method, including staff desk, public computer, and remote placement."
  },
  {
   "index": 10,
   "name": "Checkout and Renewal Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide checkout and renewal reports that count transactions individually and cumulatively by staff checkout, self-checkout, staff renewal, self-checkout renewal, online renewal, and OPAC renewal."
  },
  {
   "index": 11,
   "name": "Background Report Processing",
   "description": "Long-running reports, including inactive patron reports in environments with approximately 3.5 million patrons, must execute as background operations or through a queue without occupying the user interface, and the system must cache slow activity-query results for reuse."
  },
  {
   "index": 12,
   "name": "Accounting Data Retention",
   "description": "The system must archive financial data according to state and local requirements and enforce applicable retention policies, including seven years of audit data where required."
  },
  {
   "index": 13,
   "name": "Assistive Technology Access",
   "description": "The system must satisfy Section 508 accessibility requirements and support screen readers including JAWS and NVDA, screen magnification including ZoomText, and other assistive technologies; web output must include appropriate alt tags, ARIA labels, semantic structure, and standards-compliant HTML."
  },
  {
   "index": 14,
   "name": "Branch Management Reports and Templates",
   "description": "The system must provide predefined reports and report templates needed to cover the management reporting needs of all consortium member branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 15,
   "name": "Client And Browser Access",
   "description": "The system must be accessible through a web browser or Windows-compatible client and support Microsoft Internet Explorer 6.0 or later and Mozilla Firefox 2.0 or later."
  },
  {
   "index": 16,
   "name": "Cross-Record Query Designer",
   "description": "The system must provide a user-friendly query designer across bibliographic, item, patron, transaction, financial, and other record types, with selectable fields, picklists, regular-expression choices, and Boolean operators."
  },
  {
   "index": 17,
   "name": "Data Archiving Compliance",
   "description": "Data archiving must comply with standard accounting practices and state, county, and municipal auditing requirements."
  },
  {
   "index": 18,
   "name": "Financial Report Compliance",
   "description": "Financial reports must comply with standard accounting practices and state, county, and municipal auditing requirements."
  },
  {
   "index": 19,
   "name": "Integrated Accessible System Help",
   "description": "The system must provide hierarchical, cross-linked, searchable HTML help that describes and illustrates system functions, is accessible within the system and to screen readers, and includes integrated online help for the reporting interface."
  },
  {
   "index": 20,
   "name": "Management Reporting Scope",
   "description": "The reporting functionality must support management planning, evaluation, and oversight rather than day-to-day patron-facing functions handled by the OPAC and circulation client."
  },
  {
   "index": 21,
   "name": "OPAC Preservation",
   "description": "Management and reporting functionality must not constrain, slow, or adversely affect OPAC functionality or patron access."
  },
  {
   "index": 22,
   "name": "Open-Hours Reporting Performance",
   "description": "Searches and reports must run during open or peak hours without disrupting circulation, other system functions, or the OPAC; background queues, a separate reporting database or replica, or equivalent isolation must prevent excessive contention with the production database when needed."
  },
  {
   "index": 23,
   "name": "Patron Information Removal",
   "description": "The system must remove personally identifying information about patrons from retained transaction data."
  },
  {
   "index": 24,
   "name": "Payment Audit Ledger",
   "description": "The system must maintain a payment ledger identifying the charge or charges to which each patron payment was applied so payments can be traced and reconciled."
  },
  {
   "index": 25,
   "name": "Record Data Specifications",
   "description": "The product must provide complete data specifications and dictionaries for authority, bibliographic, order, item, hold/request, and other accessed records, including field names, data types, and relationships."
  },
  {
   "index": 26,
   "name": "Relational Database Support",
   "description": "The system must use and operate with a fully relational database, support complex joins, and not require a denormalized reporting schema that can cause synchronization problems."
  },
  {
   "index": 27,
   "name": "Report Accuracy",
   "description": "Report output must be tested and demonstrated to be accurate and dependable."
  },
  {
   "index": 28,
   "name": "Report Types and Uses",
   "description": "The reporting interface must provide canned reports, noncanned on-demand reports based on preselected criteria for maintenance and collection-development activities, and open-template reports with selectable attributes for circulation, items, patrons, bills, and bibliographic records."
  },
  {
   "index": 29,
   "name": "Server Platform Support",
   "description": "The system must operate on Linux and Solaris servers, including Red Hat Enterprise Linux 5 and Solaris 10 on SPARC hardware where specified."
  },
  {
   "index": 30,
   "name": "Standards Compliant HTML",
   "description": "Web interfaces, reports, and online help must produce HTML that validates against applicable web standards."
  },
  {
   "index": 31,
   "name": "Transaction Data Retention",
   "description": "The system must retain circulation transaction data for the legally required period."
  },
  {
   "index": 32,
   "name": "Historical Reporting",
   "description": "The system must provide historical reports through preserved data or a reporting warehouse, retrieving values as they existed at a specified date or date range, including past bills and shelving locations."
  },
  {
   "index": 33,
   "name": "Material Volume Reporting",
   "description": "The system must report material volume for a specified library system or branch at the current or a historical date, including total items, new acquisitions, material added during selectable periods based on item acquisition dates such as the preceding 30, 60, or 90 days, checked-in items, checked-out items, holds, adult fiction, and board books. Checked-out items must count toward the total, and category groupings must be configurable by library system, such as fiction/non-fiction or format."
  },
  {
   "index": 34,
   "name": "Branch Capacity And Demographics",
   "description": "The reports interface must enable analysis of branch capacity and patron demographics."
  },
  {
   "index": 35,
   "name": "Inventory Capacity Reporting",
   "description": "The system must provide authorized staff, including branch managers, with inventory and operational capacity reports for monitoring collections and capacity. The capacity report must provide an all-branch view, allow drilldown to an individual branch, show capacity trends such as growth or shrinkage, support comparison for up to 286 branches, allow users to filter and sort branches by region, library type, and current load, and allow users to export capacity-view data in CSV format."
  },
  {
   "index": 36,
   "name": "Staff Workflow And Financial Reports",
   "description": "The reports interface must enable analysis of staff workflow and financial transactions."
  },
  {
   "index": 37,
   "name": "Item Use and Circulation Metrics",
   "description": "Reports must identify usage of uncataloged materials and provide total item-use counts, counts by fiscal year, month, or other usage date range, and circulation statistics including checkout, renewal, and other transactions involving uncataloged materials."
  },
  {
   "index": 38,
   "name": "Shelf Space Analysis",
   "description": "The system must provide shelf-space analysis that compares, side by side, for each material genre and format, the percentage of total circulations, the percentage of the collection, and the percentage of total shelf space so users can identify differences and outliers, with views by branch and for the library system, selectable date ranges such as Q1 or Q2, collection percentages based on item counts, circulation percentages based on checkouts, and shelf-space estimates based on item dimensions or item count when dimensions are unavailable."
  },
  {
   "index": 39,
   "name": "Batch Item Transfers",
   "description": "Authorized staff must be able to transfer selected batches of items between branches and other destinations, including mobile libraries, outreach programs, warehouses, book sales, and event locations, by changing item locations or generating printable pull lists identifying items selected for transfer; the operation must process approximately 9.6 million items without excessive database locking."
  },
  {
   "index": 40,
   "name": "Save and Share Queries",
   "description": "The system must allow staff to save queries, including transfer-candidate queries, reuse them for repeated reporting activities including monthly reuse, and make saved queries available for reuse or sharing."
  },
  {
   "index": 41,
   "name": "Select Records for Actions",
   "description": "The system must allow staff to manually select individual records from query results for subsequent administrative actions, including selecting individual titles or items from transfer-query results before executing a transfer, such as selecting 25 items from 100 candidates."
  },
  {
   "index": 42,
   "name": "Transfer Candidate Queries",
   "description": "Staff must be able to query for candidate items for transfer using criteria such as genre, collection area, and circulation inactivity, including items that have not circulated for six months."
  },
  {
   "index": 43,
   "name": "Transfer Confirmation Preview",
   "description": "Before execution, the system must show a transfer preview containing the item count and source and destination locations and require staff confirmation."
  },
  {
   "index": 44,
   "name": "Transfer Location Updates",
   "description": "The system must update the location field and associated item records for selected items moved to a new location."
  },
  {
   "index": 45,
   "name": "Transfer Reversion",
   "description": "The system must return transferred items to their original locations after a configured period or configured event, including a status change to sold or returned or checkout at the destination."
  },
  {
   "index": 46,
   "name": "Batch Item Status Conversion",
   "description": "The system must provide a workflow that converts many selected items from one status to another in a single batch, such as changing 100 missing items to discard/weed."
  },
  {
   "index": 47,
   "name": "Data Quality and Integrity Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide periodic data-quality reports identifying invalid item types, excessively long call numbers, bibliographic records without items, and other data problems."
  },
  {
   "index": 48,
   "name": "Hold and Transit Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide periodic reports for records with holds, item-level holds, in-transit items with holds, and overdue transit."
  },
  {
   "index": 49,
   "name": "Item Change and Inventory Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide reports on items that were added, deleted, edited, counted, or created within a specified date range."
  },
  {
   "index": 50,
   "name": "Item Circulation and Status Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide periodic reports for items not checked out for a configurable period, last copies, and missing items."
  },
  {
   "index": 51,
   "name": "Confirmed Purge Deletion",
   "description": "The system must require a separate confirmation before executing purge deletions."
  },
  {
   "index": 52,
   "name": "Deletion Audit Recording",
   "description": "The system must record completed and denied deletion attempts, including who performed or attempted each deletion, the record type, record ID, timestamp, user branch, library system, and, where required, the deletion reason."
  },
  {
   "index": 53,
   "name": "Protected Purge Reports",
   "description": "Reports identifying purge candidates must be read-only and must not delete or modify candidate records."
  },
  {
   "index": 54,
   "name": "Purge Candidate Identification",
   "description": "The system must identify item records eligible for purging using customizable status, duration, and circulation criteria, including missing, discard/weed, or damaged status, time in status, and no circulation for a specified period; criteria must support Boolean combinations such as missing for six months and no circulation for two years."
  },
  {
   "index": 55,
   "name": "Check-In Method Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide check-in reports that count check-ins individually and cumulatively by book drop, terminal, staff or user processing, and self-service."
  },
  {
   "index": 56,
   "name": "Circulation Attribute Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide circulation reports using patron contact information, circulation modifiers, categories, patron county, home branch, and age, as well as item Dewey classification, shelving location, and MARC fields."
  },
  {
   "index": 57,
   "name": "Inactive Patron Reporting",
   "description": "The system must provide an inactive patron report for patrons with no circulation and no electronic activity during a configurable preceding period, where the threshold supports any number of days including 30, 90, 180, and 365 and electronic activity includes OPAC or patron-account logins, online holds, online renewals, and integrated digital-resource access. The report must separate patrons with outstanding fines, allow exclusion of patrons marked do-not-disturb, VIP patrons, staff members, and patrons who opted out of communications, include each patron's current mailing address, support CSV export for upload to an outreach system, and list or group results by configurable organizational scopes including the library system, home branch, and county so users can see system totals and assign work to branches."
  },
  {
   "index": 58,
   "name": "Null and Blank Field Inclusion",
   "description": "Patron queries and reports must include records and values with null or blank fields, including patrons whose optional demographic fields are null or blank, rather than silently excluding them."
  },
  {
   "index": 59,
   "name": "Overdue and Volume Reports",
   "description": "The system must provide reports for overdue items, non-cataloged circulation, and total circulation by type."
  },
  {
   "index": 60,
   "name": "Patron Additions and Status",
   "description": "The system must provide patron reports showing additions by month, expiration date, and barred or inactive status."
  },
  {
